            {
               "text": "### The History of a Theorem\n\nThe graph in this section is a visual proof—a mathematical argument that demonstrates the proposed concept is real (<htcite0>)—of the Pythagorean Theorem.\n\n#### Where is it?\n\nHave you ever asked yourself the following question?",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "fc17254b-5b9e-458b-8406-749d8fb6778a",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection: Pythagorean Theorem</b><br /><br />Why do I need to learn the Pythagorean Theorem?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "The Pythagorean Theorem, nowadays, is used daily by many professionals, often without them even realizing it. In this section, we make its use more visible in order to respond to the reflection we have proposed. Furthermore, we will talk about a historical misconception associated with this theorem.\n\n#### The discovery\n\nWhat we call the Pythagorean Theorem cannot have received that name when it was first discovered, since it was already known before Pythagoras was born (<htcite0>).\n\nGiven this scenario, we will follow Dr. Daniel F. Mansfield, who uses the term \"diagonal triple\" to refer to the relationship between numbers that follow the same rule attributed to Pythagoras in ancient Babylonian texts, which were written in sexagesimal positional notation (<htcite1>).\n\nThe reason why we are broadening our horizons is the values on the *Plimpton 322*, tablet, which are similar to the values we obtain with Pythagorean trigonometry. Although we cannot claim that the Babylonians used trigonometry the way the Greeks did, or as we know it today, we can indeed conclude that piece *Si. 427*, which is kept at the Istanbul Archaeological Museum, proves that the Babylonians knew about the right triangle. (<htcite2>).\n\nIn addition to the Babylonians, there are also records of the right-triangle relationship in India (<htcite3>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "3d410035-deb7-4ecd-9da0-385faa97048e",
                     "text": "Shabnam Nasimi",
                     "page": "01:32 - 01:42 ",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
